.th LPR I 7/17/74
.sh NAME
lpr \*- spool files ready for printing
.sh SYNOPSIS
.bd lpr
[ -flag ... ] file ...
.sh DESCRIPTION
.it Lpr
spools the named files for printing on the local line printer.
Its action may be modified by the following flags.
.s3
.lp +11 10
.ft I
flag	meaning
.ft R
.s3
.lp +11 10
-\fBr\fR	remove file after spooling (if permissions allow).
.s3
.lp +11 10
-\fBx\fR	do not print a banner page.
.s3
.lp +11 10
-\fBe\fR\fIn\fR	\fIn\fR is a digit (0-7) indicating a priority level
(lo-hi). The default is 0.
.s3
.lp +11 10
-\fBs\fR	file is printed at low priority (level -1).
.s3
.lp +11 10
-\fBi\fR\fIstring\fR	use the \fIstring\fR (max 20 characters)
given as the banner page jobname.
Default is login name.
.s3
.lp +11 10
-\fBp\fR	push the job out of printer if last to be printed.
.s3
.lp +11 10
-\fBc\fR	make a copy of the file when spooling.
Links are used where possible.
.s3
.lp +11 10
-\fBn\fR\fIm\fR	print \fIm\fR copies of the file under the one banner page.
.i0
.dt
.s3
If no files are specified, the standard input is read and spooled.
.sh FILES
.nf
.if t .ta 1.2i
.if n .ta 14
/tmp/lpd	spool area directory
.br
/etc/passwd	for login names
.br
/etc/lpd	spool area and print manager
.fi
.sh "SEE ALSO"
lpd (VIII), prstop (I), restart (I), repeat (I)
.sh DIAGNOSTICS
yes;
but rather esoteric !
.sh AUTHOR
So many people have tinkered with
.it lpr
and
\fIlpd\fR that space denies complete acknowledgement.
However, Greg James (formerly of UNSW, now AAEC) deserves a mention.
.sh BUGS
Definitely, but rarely.
